# Build context for BOTH job images (ingestion/Dockerfile, transform/Dockerfile).
# Both are built from the repository root, so this one file protects both:
#   docker build -f ingestion/Dockerfile -t <image>:<tag> .
#   docker build -f transform/Dockerfile -t <image>:<tag> .
#
# Why it exists: .gitignore keeps secrets out of Git, but Docker does not read
# it. Without this file a careless `COPY . .` would carry .env, the virtualenv,
# .git and data/ into an image layer, and a layer cannot be un-leaked once the
# image is pushed. The point is that it holds for the Dockerfile somebody
# writes in a hurry, not only for ours.

# 1. Start from nothing, then let in only what the two images are built from.
*
!pyproject.toml
!uv.lock
!ingestion/
!transform/

# 2. Never, even inside the folders allowed above. The last matching line wins.
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/.venv
**/venv
.git
**/__pycache__
**/*.pyc
**/*.parquet
**/*credentials*.json
**/*-key.json
**/service-account*.json
**/*.tfstate
**/*.tfstate.*
transform/target
transform/dbt_packages
transform/logs
